      "hints": [
        "First divide by $5$ and exponentiate to remove the natural logarithm.",
        "Isolate $3^x$ from $4-3^x=e^{6/5}$ and check that the resulting value is positive.",
        "Take logarithms to solve $3^x=4-e^{6/5}$, then round the final value to 3 decimal places."
      ],
      "walkthrough": [
        "Divide both sides of $5\\ln(4 - 3^x) = 6$ by $5$:\n$$\\ln(4 - 3^x) = \\dfrac{6}{5} = 1.2$$",
        "Exponentiate both sides with base $\\mathrm{e}$:\n$$4 - 3^x = \\mathrm{e}^{1.2}$$",
        "Isolate $3^x$:\n$$3^x = 4 - \\mathrm{e}^{1.2}$$",
        "Calculate the numerical value of the right-hand side:\n$$\\mathrm{e}^{1.2} \\approx 3.320117$$\n$$3^x \\approx 4 - 3.320117 = 0.679883$$",
        "Take the natural logarithm of both sides:\n$$\\ln(3^x) = \\ln(0.679883)$$\n$$x\\ln 3 = \\ln(0.679883)$$",
        "Solve for $x$:\n$$x = \\dfrac{\\ln(0.679883)}{\\ln 3} \\approx \\dfrac{-0.385834}{1.098612} \\approx -0.351201$$",
        "Round to 3 decimal places:\n$$x \\approx -0.351$$"
      ],
      "checking": {
